<Page title="Privacy policy — Pudding" description="Pudding collects no personal data and has no telemetry or analytics. Learn about local storage and connections to radio stations you choose.">
  <header class="page-heading"><h1>Privacy policy</h1><p class="small-note" style="margin-top: 20px">Effective September 10, 2026</p></header>
  <div class="prose policy">
    <section><h2>About this policy</h2><p>This policy describes the Pudding desktop app for macOS, developed by Greg Smith.</p></section>
    <section><h2>We don’t collect your data</h2><p>Pudding does not collect, transmit to its developer, sell, or share personal data. It has no telemetry, analytics, advertising, tracking, or automatic crash-reporting service. You do not need an account.</p><p>Your music library, listening activity, and app settings are not sent anywhere.</p></section>
    <section><h2>What stays on your Mac</h2><p>Pudding reads the music files and folders you choose so it can browse and play your collection. Library metadata, settings, recent items, playlists, and saved stations are stored locally. Playlists are ordinary files that you control.</p><p>This local information stays on your device unless you place it in a folder managed by a cloud storage or backup provider. Any syncing or downloading performed by that provider is governed by its own settings and privacy policy.</p><p>Local information remains until you remove it. You can remove saved stations, playlists, and app data from your Mac. There is no developer-held copy to request or delete. Removing Pudding does not automatically delete your music or playlist files.</p></section>
    <section><h2>Radio and network connections</h2><p>Pudding’s network activity is limited to the radio stations and remote station playlists you choose to add or open, including the connections needed to resolve and play those streams. Local music playback does not require a network connection.</p><p>When you connect to a station or remote playlist, its provider and hosting services receive your IP address and the request needed to deliver the content. Those providers may keep connection logs under their own privacy policies. These connections go directly to the provider; they do not pass through a Pudding service.</p><p>You control which stations and remote playlists you use. Pause playback to disconnect from a radio stream, and remove a saved station when you no longer want it in your list.</p></section>
    <section><h2>If you contact us</h2><p>If you choose to post a question or bug report on GitHub, Greg can see the information you include and use it to respond or investigate. GitHub issues are public and are handled under GitHub’s privacy policy. Please do not include personal or sensitive information in an issue.</p></section>
    <section><h2>Changes and contact</h2><p>If Pudding’s privacy practices change, this page will be updated with a new effective date.</p><p>For questions about this policy, contact Greg Smith through the <a href="https://github.com/incompl/pudding/issues">Pudding issue tracker</a>.</p></section>
  </div>
</Page>
